    "q-109583": {
      "dom_id": "step-q109583",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "The second-order differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-4{e}^{t}{y}^{′}+4ty=0\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}(t)=y(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)={y}^{′}(t).$ Find the function $f(t).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′} & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{″}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=4{e}^{t}{y}^{′}-4ty.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=4{e}^{t}{x}_{2}-4t{x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=-4t{x}_{1}+4{e}^{t}{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t)=-4t{x}_{1}+4{e}^{t}{x}_{2}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
