    "q-109643": {
      "dom_id": "step-q109643",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "The third-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}-6\\sqrt{t}\\,{y}^{″}+12{y}^{′}=0,\\,y(1)=-2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=2,\\,{y}^{″}(1)=-3\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(1)=p \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3},\\, & {x}_{2}(1)=q \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=f(t),\\, & {x}_{3}(1)=r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}(t)=y(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t)={y}^{′}(t),$ ${x}_{3}(t)={y}^{″}(t),$ and $p,$ $q$ and $r$ are constants. Calculate  $(p+q-r)\\cdot f(t).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =y \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3}(t) & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also convert the initial conditions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & ={x}_{1}(1)=-2 \\\\ {y}^{′}(1) & ={x}_{2}(1)=2 \\\\ {y}^{″}(1) & ={x}_{3}(1)=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{″} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{‴}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3}, \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{‴}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=6\\sqrt{t}\\,{y}^{″}-12{y}^{′}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=6\\sqrt{t}\\,{x}_{3}-12{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(1)=-2 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3},\\, & {x}_{2}(1)=2 \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=-12{x}_{2}+6\\sqrt{t}\\,{x}_{3},\\, & {x}_{3}(1)=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $p=-2,q=2,r=-3,f(t)=-12{x}_{2}+6\\sqrt{t}\\,{x}_{3},$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(p+q-r)\\cdot f(t) & =(-2+2-(-3))(-12{x}_{2}+6\\sqrt{t}\\,{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =(-2+2+3)(-12{x}_{2}+6\\sqrt{t}\\,{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =3(-6)(2{x}_{2}-\\sqrt{t}\\,{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =-18(2{x}_{2}-\\sqrt{t}\\,{x}_{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
